The process of tokenizing real-world assets (RWA) seems to be gaining traction, but much of its potential is still behind a veil of the traditional regulatory frameworks.
The tokenised funds usually consist of treasury securities and the substantive opportunities are limited by minimum participation requirement, storage logistics and middlemen intervention.

@ColbFinance takes a different path and does away with default custody schemes in favour of on-chain subscription systems anchored on trust-secured reserves.
The participants receive tokens of the USC, invest in structured products and thus can obtain access to asset classes that are, traditionally, closed to the participants.

Such a model is being run without traditional banking intermediaries and siloed frameworks that facilitate the effortless movement of real-life assets onto the blockchain in compliance with standard operations protocols.

